const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-of/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "MusclePharm Combat Protein Review",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/musclepharm-combat-protein-powder-review/`,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is MusclePharm Combat protein good?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, for the money. The original Combat gives 25 g of protein per scoop from a whey, milk protein and egg blend, includes lactase, and appears in the Informed Sport database. At about $1.35 per 25 g in the 4.2 lb tub at the price we saw, it is cheaper than Gold Standard. The trade-offs are sucralose, artificial flavors and a less efficient scoop."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the difference between Combat Protein and Combat 100% Whey?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Combat Protein is a five-source blend of whey concentrate, milk protein concentrate, whey isolate, whey hydrolysate and egg albumin, with lactase and protease enzymes and 25 g of protein per 37 g scoop. Combat 100% Whey uses whey concentrate and isolate only, has no enzymes and gives 24 g per 34 g scoop. Both use the same sweeteners."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is MusclePharm Combat third-party tested?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Both Combat powders appear in the Informed Sport database at the time of writing, which means certified batches are tested for banned substances. Combat 100% Whey batches tested before January 14, 2025 were Informed Choice certified. Retailers also claim Informed Protein for the blend, which we could not verify. Athletes should check their batch number."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is MusclePharm still in business?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, under new ownership. MusclePharm filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy in December 2022, and FitLife Brands bought substantially all of its assets for about $18.5 million in October 2023. The brand now sells Combat Protein, Combat 100% Whey and the Combat Ready shake, mainly through Amazon, its own site and selected retailers."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does Combat protein have egg in it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The original Combat Protein Powder does. Egg albumin is the fifth protein in the blend, and the label lists milk, egg and soy as allergens. Combat 100% Whey does not contain egg as an ingredient, but its allergen statement notes a shared facility that processes egg, so people with a severe egg allergy should check with their doctor."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Combat good for weight loss?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It can fit a weight-loss diet. One scoop of the blend has about 140 calories with 25 g of protein, 4 g of sugar and 1.5 g of fat, per the label, so it is a filling, low-fat snack. Gold Standard and ISO100 are slightly leaner per scoop. What matters most for weight loss is your total daily calories and protein intake."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Combat better than Gold Standard?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "On price and verifiable certification, yes: Combat costs about 10 percent less per 25 g in the big tubs at the prices we saw and is listed by Informed Sport. On flavor range, scoop efficiency and mixing feedback, Gold Standard is better. Buy Combat for value, Gold Standard for variety and a leaner, thinner shake."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE_NAME, url: SITE_URL },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.brand ?? product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "musclepharm combat protein powder",
      "musclepharm combat protein review",
      "musclepharm combat 100% whey",
      "is musclepharm combat protein good",
      "musclepharm protein powder"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Whey Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-whey-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">MusclePharm Combat Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      Two Combat powders are left, a 5-protein blend and a 100% whey. Both are Informed Sport listed and both undercut Gold Standard per gram. Here is which one to buy.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#verdict">The Short Verdict</a>
      <a href="#the-brand">Who Makes MusclePharm Now</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Combat Powders and Rivals</a>
      <a href="#combat-protein-label">Combat Protein: The 5-Protein Label</a>
      <a href="#combat-whey-label">Combat 100% Whey: The Label</a>
      <a href="#blend-vs-whey">Blend or Pure Whey?</a>
      <a href="#cost-per-25g">Cost per 25 g of Protein</a>
      <a href="#testing">Testing and Certification</a>
      <a href="#taste">Taste, Mixing and Digestion</a>
      <a href="#vs-rivals">Combat vs Gold Standard and Elite</a>
      <a href="#who-should-skip">Who Should Skip Combat</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          MusclePharm Combat is one of the cheapest certified proteins you can buy on Amazon right now. The original Combat Protein Powder blends five proteins (whey concentrate, milk protein concentrate, whey isolate, whey hydrolysate and egg white) into 25 g of protein per 37 g scoop, per the label at the time of writing. At the $69.99 we saw for the 4.2 lb tub, that is about $1.35 per 25 g of protein, roughly 10 percent cheaper than Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard. Buy the blend for value and its lactase enzyme; buy Combat 100% Whey only if you specifically want whey and no egg.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict
          </h2>

          <p>
            MusclePharm Combat is a good, cheap, certified protein, and it is better value than its reputation suggests. Both powders still on sale, the original Combat Protein Powder and Combat 100% Whey, cost about $1.30 to $1.40 per 25 g of protein in their big tubs at the Amazon prices we saw. That undercuts Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard and Dymatize Elite, and both Combat powders appear in the Informed Sport database, which few budget proteins can claim.
          </p>

          <p>
            The original Combat Protein Powder is the one to buy for most people. It is a blend of five proteins, it includes lactase and protease enzymes, and its 4.2 lb and 6.2 lb tubs are the cheapest way into the range. Combat 100% Whey only makes sense if you specifically want a whey-only product, for example because you avoid egg, and even then it lacks the lactase enzyme the blend carries.
          </p>

          <p>
            The trade-offs are real but modest. Both powders use sucralose and acesulfame potassium plus artificial flavors, so they are out for anyone avoiding artificial sweeteners. Both carry more non-protein material per scoop than Gold Standard: a 37 g scoop for 25 g of protein in the blend, a 34 g scoop for 24 g in the whey. And the blend contains milk, egg and soy, which rules it out for a lot of allergy profiles.
          </p>

          <p>
            In short: buy Combat Protein Powder in the 4.2 lb or 6.2 lb tub if you want the cheapest certified protein on Amazon and you are fine with sucralose. Buy Gold Standard if you want more flavors and a leaner scoop for about 10 percent more. Buy a pure isolate if whey concentrate upsets your stomach. Our <a href="/best-whey-protein-powder/">best whey protein powder rankings</a> compare all of them side by side.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE VERDICT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Cheap, certified and better than its old reputation.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Buy the original 5-protein Combat in the 4.2 lb or 6.2 lb tub. It costs about $1.30 to $1.35 per 25 g at the prices we saw, carries Informed Sport listing and includes lactase. Pick Combat 100% Whey only if you need whey without egg.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="the-brand" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Who Makes MusclePharm Now
          </h2>

          <p>
            MusclePharm was founded in Denver in 2008 by former NFL player Brad Pyatt and partners, and it grew fast on celebrity endorsements, including deals with Arnold Schwarzenegger and Tiger Woods, according to trade press coverage of the company. Combat Protein Powder became its best-known product, sold in big tubs at GNC, Costco, Walmart and grocery chains as well as online. Revenue peaked at about $166 million in 2015, the same year the company lost more than $51 million.
          </p>

          <p>
            The corporate history after that was rough, and it is worth knowing if you remember the brand from a decade ago. MusclePharm paid $700,000 in 2015 to settle Securities and Exchange Commission charges over how it reported executive perks, Pyatt stepped down as chief executive in 2016, the SEC later charged executives over inflated quarterly revenue, and the company filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy in Nevada in December 2022. None of that was about what was in the tub, but it explains why the brand faded from store shelves.
          </p>

          <p>
            In October 2023 FitLife Brands, an Omaha, Nebraska supplement company, closed its purchase of substantially all of MusclePharm's assets out of bankruptcy for about $18.5 million, without taking on the old company's liabilities. FitLife said it planned to grow the brand through direct-to-consumer online sales. That is visible today: MusclePharm's own site lists only Combat Protein, Combat 100% Whey and the Combat Ready shake, and some of its product links now redirect straight to the Amazon listings.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the Combat you buy today is the same product line under a new, smaller owner, sold mainly through Amazon and a handful of retailers such as Vitacost and Walmart. Older Combat spin-offs you may remember are not on the brand's current protein page, so treat any third-party listing for them with suspicion and check the date code. We have not tasted or tested any Combat product; nutrition figures in this review come from current labels and retailer listings, and taste notes are buyer feedback.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Combat Powders and the Rivals Worth Comparing
          </h2>

          <p>
            MusclePharm's own protein page lists just two powders today, Combat Protein and Combat 100% Whey, plus the Combat Ready shake. Both powders are sold on Amazon, and the brand's site now sends some product links straight to its Amazon listings. We have added the two rivals Combat buyers compare it with most, plus a pure isolate for anyone whose stomach does not handle concentrate. Prices are the Amazon prices we saw at the time of writing and change often. We have not tasted these powders; figures come from labels and retailer listings.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="combat-protein-label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Combat Protein Powder: The 5-Protein Label
          </h2>

          <p>
            The original Combat is the product most people mean when they say MusclePharm Combat. Per the Chocolate Milk label at the time of writing, one 37 g scoop gives the figures below. Other flavors and older labels vary slightly; some listings show a 36 g scoop at 130 calories, so check the panel on your tub. Our <a href="/protein-powder/musclepharm-combat-protein/">Combat Protein label page</a> keeps the full record.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Per 37 g scoop</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Chocolate Milk</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What it means</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 68 percent of the scoop by weight</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calories</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">140</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Typical for a whey blend</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Carbs / sugar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6 g / 4 g (0 g added)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sugar comes from the milk proteins and cocoa</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.5 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sodium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">160 mg</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Higher than most wheys, from added sea salt</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calcium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">380 mg (29% DV)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A real bonus from milk protein concentrate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">BCAAs / glutamine</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 g / 5 g (brand)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Partly from added free amino acids</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The protein comes from a microfiltered blend of whey protein concentrate, milk protein concentrate, whey protein isolate, whey protein hydrolysate and egg albumin, listed in that order. Order matters: concentrate leads, so this is mostly a concentrate-based product with isolate, hydrolysate and egg in supporting roles. Milk protein concentrate brings casein, which digests more slowly than whey, so the blend sits between a pure whey and a pure casein. If you want a slow protein for bedtime, a dedicated <a href="/best-casein-protein-powder/">casein powder</a> is still the better tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            After the proteins come Dutch-process cocoa, natural and artificial flavors, inulin (a prebiotic fiber), sea salt, soy lecithin, sucralose, acesulfame potassium, L-glutamine, potassium citrate, L-leucine, the enzymes protease and lactase, L-isoleucine, L-valine and silica. The enzymes are a genuine plus. Lactase helps break down the lactose that concentrate carries, which is why some buyers who get bloated on cheap concentrate do better on Combat.
          </p>

          <p>
            The free amino acids deserve a plain explanation. MusclePharm adds L-glutamine and the three branched-chain amino acids as separate ingredients, which is how it reaches 5 g of each per scoop. Free amino acids are not a problem in themselves, but if they were counted toward the 25 g protein figure they would inflate it, a practice called amino spiking. The label does not say how they are counted. Retailer listings claim the product carries Informed Protein, a newer program that checks protein label claims, which is reassuring, though we could not load its registry page to confirm.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="combat-whey-label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Combat 100% Whey: The Label
          </h2>

          <p>
            Combat 100% Whey is MusclePharm's answer to Gold Standard: whey only, no egg, no milk protein concentrate. Per the Chocolate Milk label at the time of writing, a 34 g scoop gives 24 g of protein and 140 calories, with 4 g of carbohydrate, 1 g of fiber, 2 g of sugar (0 g added), 3.5 g of fat including 2 g saturated, 125 mg of cholesterol and 70 mg of sodium. The 5 lb tub holds about 67 servings in chocolate and about 69 to 70 in most other flavors.
          </p>

          <p>
            The ingredient list is shorter than the blend's: whey protein concentrate and isolate, cocoa, natural and artificial flavors, xanthan gum, cellulose gum, guar gum, sucralose, acesulfame potassium and a dried cream extract that includes maltodextrin, butter and buttermilk powder. The cream extract helps explain the higher fat and cholesterol figures compared with the blend. The retailer allergen statement lists milk and soy and notes a shared facility with egg, wheat, peanuts, tree nuts and shellfish.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two details separate it from the original Combat. First, there are no added enzymes, so there is no lactase to help with the lactose in the concentrate. Second, there are three thickening gums, which is unusual for a plain whey and gives a thicker shake. The upside is that it has no added free amino acids on the ingredient list; the 4 g of glutamine the label shows is described as naturally occurring in the whey.
          </p>

          <p>
            The protein share is about 71 percent of the scoop by weight, slightly better than the blend's 68 percent and a little below Gold Standard's roughly 77 percent. That gap is not a quality problem, it simply means more flavor, gum and cream per scoop. Flavors on Amazon at the time of writing include Chocolate Milk, Vanilla, Cookies 'N' Cream, Cappuccino, Strawberry Cream and Banana Milk, all in the 5 lb size.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="blend-vs-whey" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Blend or Pure Whey: Which Combat to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            The blend versus pure whey argument is mostly marketing on both sides. A protein blend that pairs fast whey with slower casein and egg spreads amino acid delivery over a few more hours, which MusclePharm sells as fast and slow digesting. Pure whey hits the bloodstream faster and has slightly more leucine per gram. In practice, total daily protein matters far more than either effect, and research on timing shows only small differences when daily intake is adequate. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein you need</a> covers the targets.
          </p>

          <p>
            So choose on the practical differences instead. The blend is cheaper per 25 g in its bigger tubs, it contains lactase, and it delivers 380 mg of calcium per scoop. The 100% whey avoids egg and milk protein concentrate, has no added free amino acids, and comes in more flavors in a single size. Both have the same sweeteners, the same certification status and a similar 140-calorie scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pick the blend if you want the best value, you drink shakes at random times of day rather than strictly after training, or you have mild lactose trouble with cheap concentrate. Pick Combat 100% Whey if you are allergic to egg, if you simply prefer a whey-only ingredient list, or if you want Cappuccino or Banana Milk, which the blend does not offer at the time of writing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pick neither if you are genuinely lactose intolerant. Both are concentrate-led products, and lactase in the blend reduces but does not remove the issue. A whey isolate such as Dymatize ISO100 or one of the options in our <a href="/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/">lactose-free protein powder guide</a> is the safer route, and our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate explainer</a> sets out how much lactose each form carries.
          </p>

