        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Testing, Certification and Trust
          </h2>

          <p>
            Testing is Combat's quiet strength. Both powders appear in the Informed Sport database at the time of writing. Informed Sport is run by the anti-doping lab LGC and tests products for substances banned in sport, with each certified batch listed by number. The Combat 100% Whey entry notes that batches tested before January 14, 2025 were certified under the related Informed Choice program and batches tested after that date are Informed Sport, so older tubs may carry the Informed Choice logo instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the original Combat, the Informed Sport database search results list Chocolate, Vanilla, Banana, Chocolate Milk, Cookies and Cream and Horchata in 2 lb and 4 lb sizes. Retailer listings also claim Informed Protein, LGC's program for checking protein content claims. An Informed Protein registry page for Combat Protein Powder exists, but it would not load for us, so treat that claim as the retailer's, not ours. MusclePharm's own pages still use older Informed Choice wording in places.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are a drug-tested athlete, do not rely on the logo on the tub or on this review. Look up the batch number printed on your container in the Informed Sport database before you use it, because certification covers specific batches, not a brand forever. Our <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">NSF certified protein powder guide</a> explains how NSF Certified for Sport and Informed Sport compare if your league names one of them.
          </p>

          <p>
            We could not find a recent Labdoor, Clean Label Project or Consumer Reports test of either Combat powder, so we have no independent heavy-metal figures to report. That is not unusual for a budget whey; dairy-based proteins generally test lower for heavy metals than plant proteins. The corporate turmoil described above was about accounting and contracts, not product safety, and we found no recall of Combat powder in our searches. Being certified for banned substances does not mean a product is tested for every contaminant, so that gap is worth knowing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="taste" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Taste, Mixing and Digestion: What Buyers Report
          </h2>

          <p>
            We have not tasted Combat, so this section summarizes buyer feedback and published reviews. The original blend has long been known for dessert-style flavors and a creamy, slightly thick shake, helped by the milk protein concentrate and egg. Cookies 'N' Cream, Chocolate Milk and the newer Horchata come up most often as favorites in buyer reviews. Complaints focus on sweetness from the sucralose and on some foam after shaking.
          </p>

          <p>
            Combat 100% Whey gets more mixed feedback on texture. One published review of the Chocolate Milk flavor described it as rich and malty, closer to dark chocolate than milk chocolate, good in milk but watery in water, and noted that it mixed only fine, with some granules and settling after a few minutes. The three gums make it thicker than most plain wheys, which some buyers like and others find gluey. A blender bottle with a mixing ball helps with both powders.
          </p>

          <p>
            Digestion is where the two powders differ most. The blend's lactase and protease enzymes are there to ease lactose and protein breakdown, and buyers with mild sensitivity to concentrate often report fewer issues. Combat 100% Whey has no enzymes, and that same published review suggested people with lactose sensitivity avoid it. If you get gas or bloating from whey, try the blend first, then move to an isolate if it persists.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sweetener sensitivity is the other common complaint. Both powders use sucralose plus acesulfame potassium, and both list artificial flavors. If that combination gives you stomach trouble, or you simply dislike the aftertaste, Combat is not for you, and our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-artificial-sweeteners/">guide to protein powder without artificial sweeteners</a> lists stevia, monk fruit and unflavored options. For a cheap whey with a different flavor profile, the <a href="/body-fortress-protein-powder-review/">Body Fortress review</a> covers another budget alternative.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-rivals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Combat vs Gold Standard and Dymatize Elite
          </h2>

          <p>
            Against <a href="/optimum-nutrition-protein-powder-review/">Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard</a>, Combat wins on price and on clearly listed certification, and loses on flavor range and scoop efficiency. Gold Standard gives 24 g of protein in a 31 g scoop with about 120 calories and roughly 1 g of sugar per the label on our <a href="/protein-powder/optimum-nutrition-gold-standard-whey/">Gold Standard label page</a>, and it comes in more than 20 flavors. Combat costs about 10 percent less per gram in the big tubs at the prices we saw.
          </p>

          <p>
            ON describes Gold Standard as banned-substance tested and has promoted Informed Choice on many flavors, but certification varies by flavor and market. Combat's Informed Sport listing is easier to verify. If you are a casual lifter who wants a specific flavor such as Rocky Road or Cinnamon Roll, Gold Standard is worth the premium. If you want the cheapest certified tub and are happy with five or six flavors, buy Combat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against <a href="/dymatize-protein-powder-review/">Dymatize Elite 100% Whey</a>, the comparison is closer to like for like with Combat 100% Whey. Elite gives 25 g of protein in a 36 g scoop with about 140 calories, 3 g of fat and the same sucralose and acesulfame potassium sweeteners, per the label on our <a href="/protein-powder/dymatize-elite-100-whey/">Elite label page</a>. At the $109.61 we saw for 63 servings, Elite costs about $1.74 per 25 g, roughly 25 percent more than Combat 100% Whey, for one extra gram of protein per scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dymatize's stronger card is ISO100, a hydrolyzed whey isolate that suits lactose-sensitive buyers far better than any Combat product, at a much higher price. Against the other grocery-aisle blend, the <a href="/muscle-milk-protein-powder-review/">Muscle Milk review</a> shows a similar multi-protein approach with less clear certification. And if you want the egg part of the blend without the dairy, our <a href="/best-egg-white-protein-powder/">egg white protein guide</a> covers pure egg options, though none come close to Combat on price per gram.
