        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Who Should Skip Combat
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip the original Combat if you have an egg allergy, a milk allergy or a soy allergy: the blend contains all three. Combat 100% Whey drops the egg but still contains milk, and its allergen statement lists soy and a shared facility with egg, peanuts, tree nuts, wheat and shellfish. Anyone with a severe allergy should read the current label on the tub rather than a retailer summary.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip both if you are lactose intolerant rather than mildly sensitive, if you avoid artificial sweeteners or flavors, or if you want a short, clean ingredient list. Combat is a mainstream gym protein with gums, sweeteners, added amino acids and flavoring, and it does not pretend to be anything else. A plain isolate or an unflavored whey will suit those buyers better and cost only a little more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip the 2 lb tubs if you already know you like Combat. At about $1.79 per 25 g, the small tub costs more per gram than Gold Standard in a 5 lb tub, which wipes out Combat's main advantage. The same goes for the Combat Ready shakes as a daily habit; they are a convenience product priced like one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, health caveats apply as they do to any protein powder. This review is not medical advice. If you have kidney disease, are pregnant or breastfeeding, take prescription medication or have a diagnosed food allergy, talk to a doctor or registered dietitian before adding a protein supplement. For most healthy adults, one or two scoops a day to reach a sensible protein target is well within the normal range.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the 2 lb tub to save money</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 2 lb Combat tub costs about $1.79 per 25 g at the price we saw, a third more than the 4.2 lb tub and more than Gold Standard in a 5 lb tub. Buy the small tub only to test a flavor, then move to the 4.2 lb or 6.2 lb size.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Combat 100% Whey is easier on lactose</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                It sounds purer, but Combat 100% Whey is concentrate-led and has no lactase enzyme. The original blend includes lactase. If lactose is your issue, try the blend first, and if that still causes bloating, switch to a whey isolate.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting an old certification logo</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Certification covers specific batches. Combat 100% Whey moved from Informed Choice to Informed Sport for batches tested after January 14, 2025. Drug-tested athletes should check the batch number on their own tub in the Informed Sport database before using it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the egg in the blend</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The original Combat contains egg albumin as well as milk and soy. People with an egg allergy often miss it because the tub is marketed as a whey product. Combat 100% Whey is the egg-free option, but still read the shared-facility warning.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing price per tub instead of per gram</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tub prices hide serving counts and scoop sizes. Divide price by servings, then by grams of protein, and multiply by 25. Done that way, the $69.99 Combat tub beats a $105.99 Gold Standard tub, but only because it is the bigger, cheaper size.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
